WEBVTT

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Whitney Hodges: Hey, Steve, can you hear me?

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yeah, can you hear me?

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Whitney Hodges: I can. How are you?

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Steve’s iPhone: Okay, cool. Good. How are you?

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Whitney Hodges: Good. So sorry to keep you waiting. I've had this, like, domino effect today of, like, each meeting running late, and I've been back-to-back, so I'm sorry to keep you waiting.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Steve’s iPhone: Ah, no worries.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Whitney Hodges: Are you on the drive home right now?

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Steve’s iPhone: Hell yeah.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, awesome. Okay, well, I'm glad that we can connect and, um, catch up. Um, from what I saw on my end, August looked like it was a really awesome month. Um, how were things for you guys?

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Steve’s iPhone: Good. Uh, yeah, just really, really busy right now.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, on the fulfillment side.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Steve’s iPhone: Kind of scrambling.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Steve’s iPhone: There.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, good to hear. So, I know, like, I feel like when we last talked, you said July was your, you know, highest revenue month. Did August beat it, I assume?

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yes. Yes, it did.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, awesome. Good to hear. Um, and I hear what you're saying about kind of, like, being busy. Um, do you feel like, kind of, like, the amount of orders that you had in August, like, that felt good? You kind of, like, want to stay around that?

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Whitney Hodges: pace for a while? Or what are we thinking as far as growth at this point?

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yeah, I mean, I think we're obviously — we don't want to turn down any work.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Steve’s iPhone: Um…

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Steve’s iPhone: And…

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Steve’s iPhone: We're kind of at, like, an unknown, because I'm not really putting any stock into last year's numbers, because of how few we did.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Whitney Hodges: No.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.000
Whitney Hodges: Right.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Steve’s iPhone: Um, so, um…

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Steve’s iPhone: The two thoughts would be, like…

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Steve’s iPhone: I don't know what.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Steve’s iPhone: like, quote-unquote, non-wedding season market is gonna look like for us. I don't know if it, like, if we're gonna be, like, more or less seasonal, like, kind of the wedding industry. Um, at least in, like, the Philadelphia area, there's not really too many weddings in, like, winter and, like, super early spring.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:17.000
Steve’s iPhone: Um, so I don't know if that's the same around the country. Um, if it is, then maybe we'll die down a little bit, but…

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Steve’s iPhone: Um, I guess with all the marketing that we've been doing.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Steve’s iPhone: Pretty much, I would… honestly, I think, like, 90…

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Steve’s iPhone: Percent of the bookings that we've gotten…

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Steve’s iPhone: that we got in August were for…

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Steve’s iPhone: September and October.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Whitney Hodges: Wow. Uh-huh.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Steve’s iPhone: So, just the quick turnaround is, like…

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Steve’s iPhone: it really kind of just ramped up everything, like, I have, like, like, 3 new editors I'm trying to, like, train and bring on at the same time of, like, our 2-week turnaround time, which is so quick, it's just… everything is kind of just starting to hit at the same time, and…

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Steve’s iPhone: Um, it's just kind of a lot at the moment, and obviously still trying to maintain the quality that we're used to.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, yeah, no, that totally makes sense. I hear you there.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Whitney Hodges: So taking all of that into account and kind of what we're seeing on the the ad side. So we did just up the budget on the Meta ad side today. Really putting like more dedicated budget to that Ugc ad in particular.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yep.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:41.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, to kind of see if we can get it to take off, because, I mean, I think based on what you've seen with it, based on what I've seen with it, like, the creative is great, um, so I want to give it more of, like, a fair shot, right? Um, like, force Meta to feed it out to people, and then kind of, like, go from there before we make any…

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Steve’s iPhone: Sure, yeah.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Whitney Hodges: decisions on that. So.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.000
Whitney Hodges: Upping the volume on Meta certainly could, you know, impact.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:04:09.000
Whitney Hodges: sales volume a bit. So we'll kind of see how that pans out for the month of September, kind of this latter half of September. What we're seeing on the Meta side in general. Did you check out the report that I sent your way by any chance? Did you have a chance to look at it?

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.000
Steve’s iPhone: When you said it, I kind of just like briefly scroll through it.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:19.000
Steve’s iPhone: I didn't like to dive down into it or like really look into it too much, to be honest.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, no worries. The thing to kind of keep in mind with the Meta ads is that, like, when we look inside your Meta ads manager, like, we're seeing a great cost per purchase, we're seeing purchase conversions happen there, but Meta has a 7-day attribution window.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:51.000
Whitney Hodges: So, if somebody sees your ad, clicks on it, right, and then 7 days later, purchases, right, say that they Google you, and they come through a Google ad, or they come through Google Organic, or they just, like.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:05:08.000
Whitney Hodges: go into, um, you know, a tab on their browser and type in pressrecordco.com, Meta will attribute that as a purchase conversion, but on the Shopify side, it's not going to. Shopify is going to say, you know, wherever it came from as far as, like.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.000
Steve’s iPhone: Right.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:24.000
Whitney Hodges: last click. So, the thing to keep in mind, it's not like an apples-to-apples sort of thing, it's more like Meta is, like, impacting things holistically, right? But the numbers don't, like, fully match up there, sort of thing. So that's just…

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.000
Steve’s iPhone: Right.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yes.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Whitney Hodges: one thing to keep in mind there, versus on, like, the Google ad side, where, yes, in the Google, um, search campaign, you know, the attributions there are higher than what we see inside of Shopify, but there's not as much of a…

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.000
Whitney Hodges: discrepancy. If if that makes sense.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:06:00.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, so, all that to say, I think we're on the right track with the meta ads. We'll kind of see what comes about with this, you know, additional budget allocation. Now, on the Google ad side, we did launch that additional campaign that's more, like, videography.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yes.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:16.000
Whitney Hodges: keyword focused, that's for the Philly area, um, that's kind of trying to push, um, like that, the hybrid packages as you, as you, right? And that really hasn't gotten any traction. So, like, cost per click has been high.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:10.000
Steve’s iPhone: Right.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.000
Steve’s iPhone: Mm-hmm.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:15.000
Steve’s iPhone: Okay.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:32.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, from the people who do click and kind of have landed on… we did a split test, so we've sent some people to the homepage, we've sent other people to more of, like, the packages page, just to see if there's any sort of difference between the two, but nobody has clicked that inquire button.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.000
Whitney Hodges: Yet.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.000
Steve’s iPhone: Okay.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:51.000
Whitney Hodges: It's close to having spent $300 or so at this point, really without seeing any results. I would recommend just turning it off entirely. Um, the way that we could go about that in the future, if you're interested, would be if you created, like, a dedicated landing page.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yep.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.000
Steve’s iPhone: That's fine.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:07:06.000
Whitney Hodges: the website that really talks specifically about that service and the campaign go to that. So that would be how I would kind of recommend tackling that if you want to kind of go down that road in the future.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yes.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:22.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yeah, that sounds good. It was kind of just like a shot in the dark to see what… see if it stuck. Uh, really just relying on, like, the people that are coming for the normal rental, just so they kind of be intrigued about it anyway. So, I think the… we just kind of keep going that route, that'll be fine.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:12.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:37.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, okay, sounds good. Um, but the… the Google search campaign itself, you know, which we're… we've been, um, scaling, I don't know that it's fully up to $1,000 a month yet, but we're still kind of, like, working towards that. Um, it's continuing to do.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:53.000
Whitney Hodges: really well. Um, I went ahead and pulled the Shopify numbers, um, earlier today, just to kind of see, like, the revenue breakdown. Um, Google Ads for this month are in the lead, in terms of, like, what's generating the most revenue, and then Google Organic.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:08:00.000
Whitney Hodges: is right behind it. Now, one thing that we are seeing.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:10.000
Whitney Hodges: is that there have been a few Google Ads purchases that have been attributable to people searching.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:20.000
Whitney Hodges: for Press Record Co. Like, they're typing in your name, right? So, wanted to kind of see how you felt about that, right? Because, like.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.000
Steve’s iPhone: Right.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:36.000
Whitney Hodges: you know, somebody without running Google Ads that show up for Press Record Co. Like, in theory, they should be able to type that in, and you're gonna just show up organically, right, through the website. Um, but at this point.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:51.000
Whitney Hodges: we don't, like, we don't have Press Record Co. as a keyword that we're bidding on specifically, but some of… because of some of the broad match keywords, it's showing the Google Ads, it's serving it to people when they search that. Um…

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:58.000
Whitney Hodges: What we can do, if you'd like, is we, like, we could go down the road of adding.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:05.000
Whitney Hodges: the brand name as a negative keyword so that your ad doesn't show up when people search the name.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:04.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yep.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:09.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yeah, I mean, how much are we talking that it's, like, cost us so far?

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:27.000
Whitney Hodges: Let me pull those exact numbers for you. I mean, it's… it's not like it's all of it by any means, right? Like, it's… it is just a portion, and I think what we've seen is that there's been a bit of an uptick the month of September versus October, so let me pull just a little bit more data on that so that we can make a bit more of, like.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:21.000
Steve’s iPhone: Right.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.000
Whitney Hodges: an informed decision there.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.000
Steve’s iPhone: There.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:31.000
Whitney Hodges: Um…

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:36.000
Whitney Hodges: you know, you could make the argument either way, right? Because it's like, if you're showing up.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:49.000
Whitney Hodges: with a paid result and a organic result. It just like gives even more authority. The way that the paid result reads is a little different than the organic result like it is.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.000
Steve’s iPhone: Right.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:57.000
Whitney Hodges: it's just more conversion-focused in terms of, like, telling, like, take the next step and, like, what the benefits are. Um…

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:53.000
Steve’s iPhone: Right.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.000
Whitney Hodges: So, yeah, let me pull…

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:04.000
Steve’s iPhone: I mean, we're talking, like, probably, like, a couple bucks to, like, $20, $30 a month.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:05.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:08.000
Steve’s iPhone: We're not talking, like, 100, 100 plus dollars a month, right?

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.000
Whitney Hodges: Um…

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:20.000
Whitney Hodges: I think I did actually, in kind of, like, digging into it, I think it was around $100 a month at this point that potentially could be saved, right, if we were to…

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:28.000
Steve’s iPhone: Right. Yeah, because the idea is that, like, that's already, like, a high conversion, because they're basically looking for us on Google.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:41.000
Whitney Hodges: Right, right, yeah, and so we, like, we weren't bidding on your brand as a keyword, but, like, because of some broad match stuff, it's, like, um, been shown to people, like, Google's been starting to do that, so if we want to stop it.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:30.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yeah, I gotcha.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:33.000
Steve’s iPhone: Right.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:44.000
Whitney Hodges: We can.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:53.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yeah, I guess my question would be, is that, like, kind of, like, a negative impact on anything else, or is it just that people search Press Record Co. in the actual Google search bar?

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:54.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:56.000
Steve’s iPhone: Or just organic will come up.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:59.000
Whitney Hodges: Right, yes. And so, and…

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:09.000
Whitney Hodges: So long as your competitors aren't bidding on Press Record Co. at this point, like, that could probably be an argument for keeping it on, you know what I mean?

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:09.000
Steve’s iPhone: Alright.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:14.000
Whitney Hodges: Let me see if we can look into if any of those competitors are.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:28.000
Whitney Hodges: bidding on… on your name at this point. I mean, I just kind of did a search on my own earlier today, um, and didn't see anybody else's ads pop up, um, but I think that would be, like, the one reason to… to keep it going, or…

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yeah.

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:24.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yeah.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:36.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yeah. Yeah, I think that would be the differentiation, is if they are, then I would say, whatever, just keep it. But if they're not, I mean, I'd rather $100 a month go towards.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.000
Whitney Hodges: Yes.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.000
Steve’s iPhone: Trying to get anyone else.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:41.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, people that might… haven't heard of you before, right?

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:41.000
Steve’s iPhone: Right.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:43.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, okay, totally makes sense.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:54.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, yeah, so those were the big things that I'm seeing on my end that I wanted to make sure to kind of talk out with you. Um, any other questions or concerns on your end?

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.000
Steve’s iPhone: Uh, no, I don't think so.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:01.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, awesome. Um…

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:15.000
Whitney Hodges: Well, congrats! It's super exciting to, like, see your, your growth, um, you know, month, month after month, um, and, um, I know you're super busy, so hang in there and let us know what we can do to support.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:22.000
Steve’s iPhone: Cool, yeah, sounds good. I'm just really… I think we're… the next step is really just to kind of see how the winter goes.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:25.000
Steve’s iPhone: Um, and then we kind of just go from there.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:36.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, sounds perfect. All right. Well, yeah, I'll pull together some more information for you just as far as the, those branded searches, and we can go from there in terms of making adjustments.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:42.000
Steve’s iPhone: Cool, yeah, and then I think, um, the general plan would be to kind of just run through this.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:47.000
Steve’s iPhone: Um, current plan through the end of the year. Um, and maybe we'll have, like, uh…

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:45.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:51.000
Steve’s iPhone: Like, a full year-end review, and see, kind of.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:13:06.000
Steve’s iPhone: if we want to keep the same budget, if we want to allocate things elsewhere, if we want to up things, um, and just kind of stuff like that, just to kind of give you, like, an idea of what we're going to be looking for, maybe, like, the end of December, early, uh, January.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:16.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, perfect. Yeah, that's really helpful. I think, given how busy you guys are right now, it makes sense to not, like, continue to scale the budget, for sure.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:22.000
Steve’s iPhone: Yeah, yeah, we'll kind of see how the winter goes. If it stays the same, then obviously we'll just kind of just keep everything the same.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:23.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:32.000
Steve’s iPhone: Um, and the idea was kind of play it by ear, I guess. The winner's gonna be really, um, kind of like a big indicator of how I think, um.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:39.000
Steve’s iPhone: I guess everything goes, because we know how the busy months will go, and if the non-busy months.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:38.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:42.000
Steve’s iPhone: Stay somewhat the same, then…

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:46.000
Steve’s iPhone: We'll probably have to make some adjustments somewhere along the lines.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:14:03.000
Whitney Hodges: Right. Yeah, it definitely will be interesting to see how things change seasonally, like how the search volume changes seasonally as well. Yeah, because I know that you're, you know, slower in Philly for weddings in the winter and early spring, but.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:07.000
Whitney Hodges: It's definitely not the case around the country, you know.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.000
Steve’s iPhone: Right.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:08.000
Whitney Hodges: Yes.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:10.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, perfect.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:10.000
Steve’s iPhone: Cool.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:27.000
Whitney Hodges: So, um, before I let you go, I did just want to mention, so I know we've got this on the calendar as a monthly recurring call, um, my daughter just made the swim team, and she's gonna start to have practices, um, this day and time on Mondays.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:43.000
Whitney Hodges: So I wanted to see if there was flexibility on your end for us to maybe look at a different day of the week, or, um, different time, potentially. I mean, I know that you put your 9 to 5, so I can be flexible about that.

